\title{Overcoming outcome-label scarcity through population pretraining and adaptive sharing across haematological diseases}
\author{\placeholder{Authors and affiliations}}
\date{Working manuscript --- \today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Clinical decisions in haematology depend on predicting treatment benefit,
relapse, toxicity and survival, yet the relevant events can be scarce within an
individual disease--outcome pair. Population-scale pretraining offers reusable
representations from broad patient histories; it does not by itself establish
which labelled diseases and outcomes should inform a scarce target. We test
these complementary forms of transfer in a nationwide linked haematology resource,
we defined 24 fine disease cohorts, 69 approved outcomes and six prediction
horizons. We designed a matched readout ladder: pooled multi-horizon binary
cross-entropy with a shared representation; disease-source or outcome-source
adapters alone; and both source banks with additive or interactive routing.
All arms use the same supervised panel, allowing source-bank effects to be
distinguished from the effect of pooling training data. A separate encoder
ladder tests local pretraining, general-population PHAIR pretraining and
haematology domain adaptation under matched downstream readouts.
\placeholder{Insert locked held-out headline effects, uncertainty, rare-task
interaction and XGBoost comparison after the analysis is frozen.}
Together these experiments ask which sources of transferable information
actually reduce label scarcity without harmful transfer.
\end{abstract}

\vspace{2mm}
\noindent\textbf{Editorial summary}\quad
\textit{Population pretraining and cross-task supervised learning offer distinct
ways to address sparse disease--outcome labels. OPERA tests whether either
improves prediction where events are rare, and whether adaptive routing can
retain useful sharing without harmful transfer.}

\section*{Introduction}
Prediction informs treatment intensity, supportive care, surveillance and
trial design in haematology. The difficulty is not merely that some diseases
are rare. Once patients are divided by diagnosis, target outcome and prediction
horizon, even a large linked cohort can contain few relevant events for an
individual clinical question. Censoring and variable follow-up further limit
what can be learned and evaluated. This fragmentation of labels is a general
problem for specialized clinical prediction, not a peculiarity of haematology.
Traditional disease-specific scores, such as the NCCN International Prognostic
Index and CLL-IPI, compress a diagnostic snapshot into a transparent rule
\citep{zhou2014nccnipi,cllipi2016}. Task-specific machine learning can use
richer longitudinal histories, but usually aggregates them into fixed windows
and fits a separate predictor for each clinical target
\citep{agius2020clltim,werling2026dlbcl} (\cref{fig:paradigms}). Neither
approach automatically solves the small-label problem in a rare cell.

Population-scale pretraining is one proposed response. Sequence models learn
from ordered patient histories before specialist outcome labels are supplied
\citep{li2020behrt,rasmy2021med,odgaard2024core}; broad pretraining has shown
promise for label efficiency, including in time-to-event modelling
\citep{steinberg2023motor}. Its attraction is clear: a model trained on many
patients may carry useful structure into a disease with few labelled events.
But pretraining does not create target-event labels, and a general patient
representation need not be the best representation for every disease and
endpoint. Few-shot benchmarks and critical reviews underscore the need to
measure transfer on the particular tasks and populations where it is claimed
\citep{wornow2023ehrshot,wornow2023shaky}. The relevant question is therefore
not simply whether a large pretrained model predicts well, but when its broad
experience adds value beyond local pretraining and strong task-specific models.

A second source of information is already labelled: neighbouring diseases and
outcomes in the same care system. Multi-task and meta-learning studies show
that related prediction tasks can improve low-resource targets
\citep{harutyunyan2019multitask,zhang2019metapred}. Yet pooling labels need
not help every target. Shared supervision may transfer useful risk structure,
irrelevant structure, or both; disease and outcome relatedness cannot be
assumed from clinical labels alone. This motivates two separable tests: whether
joint training beats a specialist fitted to each supported disease--outcome
pair, and whether patient-dependent routing improves on a conventional pooled
model by selecting which disease and outcome sources to borrow from.

Our earlier gradient-boosted study in diffuse large B-cell lymphoma found that
including related aggressive lymphoma subtypes did not degrade prediction of
treatment failure, while using fixed lookback windows discarded the order of
clinical events \citep{werling2026dlbcl}. That result concerned one endpoint
in closely related diseases; it neither established a general pooling benefit
nor tested whether other outcomes can supply useful supervision. We therefore
consider not only patients, variables and longitudinal observations, but also
the breadth of disease cohorts and outcome tasks available for learning
(\cref{fig:information}). Greater breadth creates opportunities for transfer,
not a guarantee of improvement.

The central question is whether borrowing either unlabelled patient-history
structure or labelled disease--outcome experience improves prediction as target
events become scarce. We first compare pooled training against same-encoder
specialists across supported disease--outcome pairs; we then test whether
disease- and outcome-source routing improves on ordinary pooled learning. A
separate, matched encoder comparison asks whether general-population PHAIR
pretraining and local domain adaptation add to, or substitute for, supervised
sharing. Performance and failures of transfer are examined across the full
approved panel rather than inferred from a few favourable endpoints.

To answer these questions we used DALY-CARE, a nationwide linked resource that
integrates ten Danish national registers with hospital electronic health
records, laboratory results, and disease-specific quality registries for adults
diagnosed with lymphoid cancers since 2002 \citep{brieghel2025dalycare}. The
resource description reported the cohort available at the time of that report;
the present study draws a later extract, so the denominators here are not
expected to match those figures. Our study population is also defined
differently from our earlier lymphoma analysis. That study indexed at the start
of first-line therapy in aggressive lymphoma and asked one question at one
horizon, whereas the present study indexes first-line treatment episodes across
the full haematological spectrum and evaluates many endpoints at several
horizons. The two populations overlap substantially and share a common data
substrate, but they are not identical and the results are not directly
comparable patient for patient.

On this basis we defined a temporal evaluation with patients indexed from
2023 onward held out, 69 approved outcomes across 24 fine disease cohorts,
and six horizons. The primary neural comparison is a prespecified five-arm
sharing ladder: pooled shared multi-horizon BCE, disease-source routing only,
outcome-source routing only, additive routing over both banks, and interactive
routing over both banks. The source-bank contrasts isolate adaptive borrowing
\emph{conditional on pooled training}; disease-specific versus joint training
is a separate contrast. Clinical scores and task-specific XGBoost remain
external comparators once matched feature matrices are locked. The study is
also an empirical atlas of what can be predicted from longitudinal records.

