\subsection*{What is gained by foundation-model pretraining?}
The encoder-transfer analysis will compare locally pretrained Time2Vec,
general-population PHAIR initialization without local continued pretraining,
and PHAIR followed by haematology domain-adaptive pretraining, with the same
approved outcome panel and downstream readout. A randomly initialized or
local-only encoder serves as a capacity-matched reference where feasible.
This ladder separates pretraining scope from local domain adaptation; it is
not yet a completed PHAIR result. \placeholder{Insert verified PHAIR checkpoint
compatibility, matched runs, paired held-out effects and uncertainty.}

The primary contrast is general-population versus local haematology
pretraining under an otherwise matched downstream protocol. The same encoder
contrast should be examined under both the shared BCE readout and the selected
router: this tests whether better pretraining replaces, complements, or changes
the benefit of supervised adaptive sharing. We additionally test whether any
advantage is concentrated among patients who are atypical in the local
embedding distribution.
To assess whether broad pretraining changes the value of labelled-data pooling
itself, pooled shared BCE and disease--outcome specialists must also be
compared with the same PHAIR-derived encoder on both sides. This is a separate
pretraining-by-pooling interaction from the pretraining-by-router interaction;
the PHAIR specialist grid follows the same support and evaluation rules as
the local-encoder grid.

Because the case for foundation models rests largely on their behaviour when
labels are scarce, we do not report this contrast only as a single average.
Each pretraining comparison is additionally summarized as a function of cohort
size and event support, so that a benefit concentrated in well-populated
cohorts is distinguishable from one that materializes where task-specific
training is least viable.

