\subsection*{Can related cohorts compensate for scarce disease-specific data?}
For a full-panel claim about the benefit of joint training, the counterfactual
is a specialist trained on each estimable fine-disease--outcome cell, initialized
from the same encoder and evaluated against the joint model on exactly the same
patients. One specialist can predict all six horizons, so separate models for
each horizon are unnecessary. Prespecified event-support thresholds determine
which cells can sustain a specialist; unsupported cells remain visible as not
estimable. A sampled subset would answer a narrower question and cannot justify
a full-panel claim. We model paired performance differences against training
event support with partial pooling over diseases, outcomes and horizons.
\placeholder{Run and report the specialist grid, initialization and budget,
posterior joint-training effect, rare-cell interaction and failures of transfer.}

The main scarcity forest will show paired held-out differences, not absolute
AUCs alone. Its principal rows are pooled shared BCE minus the matched
disease--outcome specialist (benefit of pooling), interactive routing minus
pooled shared BCE (benefit of adaptive borrowing), and interactive routing
minus the specialist (total practical gain). Cells without an estimable
specialist are displayed separately as a coverage result, never assigned an
invented specialist contrast. A companion encoder-transfer panel compares
general-population pretraining and local domain adaptation under a fixed
readout; it is not mixed into the router contrast. The full outcome-by-outcome
forest belongs in the supplement, with the main figure emphasizing the
support gradient, clinical-family heterogeneity and lower tail of harm.
Within the routed-model panel, an equal-scale additive router is the explicit
mechanistic comparator for the interactive router. Their difference is shown
separately from the three scarcity contrasts, so interaction is not conflated
with either pooling or residual-scale capacity.

This analysis distinguishes population-level performance from task-balanced
performance. The former weights patients according to their frequency in the
registry; the latter gives each estimable cohort--outcome target equal standing
and is central to the rare-disease claim.

\subsection*{Which labelled sources should a patient borrow from?}
The main supervised comparison contains five matched readouts: pooled shared
multi-horizon BCE; a router with disease-indexed adapter sources only; a router
with outcome-indexed sources only; an additive router with both source banks;
and an interactive router with both banks
(\cref{fig:router-source-factorial,fig:source-router}). Every arm trains on the
same pooled patient and outcome panel. Here ``disease-only'' and
``outcome-only'' refer to eligible \emph{adapter banks}, not to isolated
training cohorts or outcomes. The no-source router is a matched architectural
control, distinct from plain shared BCE. Family trunks, outcome attention,
factorized readouts and other exploratory architectures are retained as
supporting screens rather than additional co-primary hypotheses
(\cref{fig:architectures}).

An earlier architecture screen used a common curriculum and hierarchical
support-weighted loss. The approved-panel router comparison uses endpoint-group
macro multi-horizon BCE and endpoint-group macro tuning AUC for checkpoint
selection. \placeholder{Insert verified completed-run roster, common-epoch and
best-checkpoint tuning summaries, paired fixed-checkpoint AUC/Brier/calibration
contrasts, source-removal effects and uncertainty.}
Because architecture changes may redistribute rather than uniformly improve
performance, the confirmatory comparison uses paired predictions and reports
the mean effect, the proportion of tasks improved, lower-tail degradation,
calibration, and heterogeneity across clinical domains. Outcome reliability
weighting and empirically derived task groupings are treated as explicit
ablations rather than embedded in the definition of the primary metric.

To separate the two proposed channels of adaptive transfer, the nested
$2\times2$ source-bank ablation (\cref{fig:router-source-factorial}) compares
neither bank, disease sources only, outcome sources only, and both banks under
the same interactive query. It is complemented by an additive-versus-interactive
contrast with both banks. The encoder, pooled data, BCE objective, selection
rule and residual scale must be matched within each contrast; otherwise a
scale effect could be mistaken for a query-interaction effect. These contrasts
estimate the added value of \emph{source banks} conditional on joint training,
not the total effect of pooling diseases or outcomes. The observed 0.1--0.3
residual-scale sweep is reported separately as a capacity sensitivity analysis,
with a fresh, code-verified 0.1 reference rather than the historical checkpoint
whose interaction branch was absent.

